        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Busca',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Busca is an Italian variation of CiapanÃ² (reverse trick-taking game) for four players. The goal is to take at least one point but avoid taking the most points. Uses a 40-card Italian or Milanese pack with unique scoring where the Ace of Coins (Pita) is worth 8 points.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Busca is a variation of CiapanÃ² (also known as Rovescino, Traversone, or Tressette a non Prendere), a reverse trick-taking game from Lombardy, Italy. CiapanÃ² was very popular in Lombardy until the 1980s and is still played there.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '4 players, each playing for themselves',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '40-card pack: Italian suits (swords, batons, cups, coins) or Milanese/French suits (spades, clubs, hearts, diamonds). Cards rank: 3 (high), 2, Ace, King, Horse/Queen, Jack/Fante, 7, 6, 5, 4 (low).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '40-card Italian or Milanese pack, score sheet for tracking busche',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Deal 10 cards to each player (anticlockwise). Turn to deal passes to the right after each hand.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'CiapanÃ² target score variants: Some groups play CiapanÃ² with a target score of 21 or 41 instead of 31, changing when players are eliminated or the match ends.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Lowest-score-wins ending: Some play that when a player reaches or exceeds the target score, the player with the lowest cumulative penalty score immediately wins.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Ace-of-clubs 21-point variant: A Traversone variant in which the Ace of clubs is worth 11 points and there are 21 total points in each deal, with cappotto allowing a large score swing.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Kifameno (Who Wins Least): Kifameno is a Slovenian losing Tressette variant where 2–4 players are dealt 10 cards each and undealt cards form a draw stack that is used between tricks.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
